ottle","oxygen is chemically removed"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Higher p(CO2) forces a larger x(CO2) in the liquid (Henry’s law).","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"henrys-law","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-so-143","chapter_id":"chemistry-solutions","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Deep-sea divers risk decompression sickness because nitrogen","options":["dissolves more at high pressure and bubbles out on a rapid ascent","is insoluble at depth","binds irreversibly to haemoglobin only","forms an azeotrope with blood plasma"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"High p(N2) loads the blood; a sudden drop in pressure releases bubbles in the tissues.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"henrys-law","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-so-144","chapter_id":"chemistry-solutions","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Warming a solvent generally","options":["raises $K_H$ and lowers gas solubility","lowers $K_H$ and raises gas solubility","leaves gas solubility untouched","ionises the dissolved gas"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Gas dissolution is exothermic, so $K_H$ increases and x falls as T rises.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"henrys-law","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-so-145","chapter_id":"chemistry-solutions","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"A compact statement of Henry’s law for a sparingly soluble gas is","options":["$p = K_H x$","$p = K_H / x$","$p = x / K_H^2$","$\\pi = CRT$